<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Perge</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 25 BCE (approx.) · capital Perge</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Perge, a Greek city of the Pamphylian plain, famous for its sanctuary of Artemis and the mathematician Apollonius.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Greek tradition says Perge was founded after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a> by heroes such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mopsus">Mopsus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calchas">Calchas</a>, and a city called Parha appears in a Hittite treaty. By the 7th century BCE it was a Greek-speaking city near the coast, inland on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kestros">Kestros</a> river.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Perge was a self-governing city whose patron was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis-pergaia">Artemis Pergaia</a>, worshipped at a rich temple. It surrendered to Alexander of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a> in 333 BCE and later passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalid-dynasty">Attalid</a> rule. The mathematician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollonius-of-perga">Apollonius of Perga</a> wrote his work on conic sections in the 3rd century BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Perge came under Roman control, and in 25 BCE it became part of the Roman province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/galatia">Galatia</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamphylia">Pamphylia</a>. Its finest monuments, including colonnaded streets and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, date from the Roman period.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/perge.jpg" alt="Perge" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Saffron Blaze · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Perge_city_overview.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 25 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Perge</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Turkey</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Perga</dd></dl></aside></div>

